          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the simplest case,
            %em tensorial strength
            is a property of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \colon V \to V
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            which characterizes it as a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor"} enriched functor
            when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is canonically regarded as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            over itself.
          %p More generally, tensorial strength on a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F \,\colon\, \mathbf{C}_0 \to \mathbf{C}_1
          %p
            between categories that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensoring"} tensored
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copowering"}> copowered
            ) over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi V
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi i
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\textstyle{\otimes}
                  \\,\colon\,
                  V \times C_i \longrightarrow C_i
          %p
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  v \otimes F(c) \to F(v \otimes c)
          %p
            exhibiting compatibility of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            with that
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensoring"} tensoring
          %p
            In the case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functors"} enriched functors
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#RelationToEnrichedFunctors"} below
          %p#WarningOnTerminology
            In fact, beware that
            %a{:href => "#Kock72"} Kock 1972
            originally said
            %em tensorial strength
            in constrast to the
            %em strength
            of
            %em strong functors
            by which he referred to
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functors"} enriched functors
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monad"} strong monad
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#RelationToEnrichedFunctors"} below
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#standard_definition Standard definition
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, \otimes, I)
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            %strong tensorial left-strength
            for a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon V \to V
          %p
            is usually defined as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi β
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo ,
                      %mi w
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi v
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\beta_{v, w}
                  \\;\colon\;
                  v \otimes F(w)
                  \\longrightarrow
                  F(v \otimes w)
          %p
            making the following
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} diagrams commute
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi u
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi v
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi w
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mrow
                                  %mi u
                                  %mo ⊗
                                  %mi v
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi w
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi u
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi v
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi w
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi α
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi u
                                    %mo ,
                                    %mi v
                                    %mo ,
                                    %mi F
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi w
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi F
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msub
                                  %mi α
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi u
                                    %mo ,
                                    %mi v
                                    %mo ,
                                    %mi w
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi u
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi v
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi w
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mn 1
                                %mi u
                              %mo ⊗
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mrow
                                  %mi v
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi w
                        %mtd
                          %mi u
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi v
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi w
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mrow
                                  %mi u
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi v
                                  %mo ⊗
                                  %mi w
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi u
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi v
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi w
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  (u \otimes v) \otimes F(w) &amp; &amp; \overset{\beta_{u\otimes v,w}}{\to} &amp; &amp; F((u\otimes v)\otimes w)\\
                  ^\mathllap{\alpha_{u,v,F(w)}}\downarrow &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{F(\alpha_{u,v,w})}}\\
                  u\otimes (v\otimes F(w)) &amp; \underset{1_u\otimes\beta_{v,w}}{\to} &amp; u\otimes F(v\otimes w) &amp; \underset{\beta_{u,v\otimes w}}{\to} &amp; F(u\otimes (v\otimes w))
                  }
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi I
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi v
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mrow
                                  %mi I
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi v
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi I
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi v
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi λ
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi F
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi v
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi F
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msub
                                  %mi λ
                                  %mi v
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi v
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  I\otimes F(v) &amp; \overset{\beta_{I,v}}{\to} &amp; F(I\otimes v) \\
                  \&amp; _\mathllap{\lambda_{F(v)}}\searrow &amp; \downarrow^\mathrlap{F(\lambda_v)}\\
                  \&amp; &amp; F(v)
                  }
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associator"} associator
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitor"} unitor
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            A
            %strong tensorial right-strength
            (not to be confused with a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensorial+costrength"} tensorial costrength
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo ,
                      %mi w
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi v
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi w
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_{v, w}: F(w) \otimes v \to F(w \otimes v)
          %p
            When
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            = succeed "." do
              %strong tensorial strengths
          %p
            A functor equipped with a tensorial strength is called a
            %strong strong functor
            (not to be confused with a
            = succeed "," do
              %em strong 2-functor
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudofunctor"} pseudofunctor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+2-functor"} lax 2-functor
            whose coherence cells are invertible).
          %p
            More generally, the notion makes sense not just for endofunctors of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed ".\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %h3#tentative_reformulation Tentative reformulation
          .standout
            %p
              %em Tentative
              – The following reformulation &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "&Baez09"}> Baez 2009
              \&amp;rbrack; of the standard definition is tentative.
          %p
            It seems we may phrase this definition more conceptually as follows. There is a bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo −
                  %mi Act
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V-Act
            where:
          %ul
            %li
              the objects are categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              equipped with a lax left
              = succeed "-action," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functor"} lax monoidal functor
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo :
                      %mi V
                      %mo →
                      %mi End
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : V \to End(C)
            %li the morphisms are functors laxly preserving this action, and
            %li the 2-morphisms are natural transformations compatible with this action.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            has a canonical left action on itself, which allows us to think of it as an object of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                    %mi Act
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V-Act
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: V \to V
            %strong tensorial strength
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is defined to be a way of equipping it with the structure of a morphism in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                    %mi Act
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V-Act
          %p A bit more precisely:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo −
                  %mi Act
                  %mo =
                  %mi Lax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V-Act = Lax(B V, Cat)
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B V
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            regarded as a 1-object weak 2-category,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            is regarded as a 2-category, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lax
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lax
            is the bicategory of
          %ul
            %li weak 2-categories
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+functor"} lax functors
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+natural+transformation"} lax natural transformations
          %p There is a forgetful 2-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo −
                  %mi Act
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V-Act \to Cat
          %p
            and a
            %strong tensorial strength
            for a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: V \to V
            is a way of lifting it to a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo :
                  %mover
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{F}: \tilde{V} \to \tilde{V}
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                    %mi Act
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V-Act
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{V}
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            equipped with its canonical left action on itself.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationToEnrichedFunctors Relation to enriched functors
          %blockquote
            %p
              (For more references see also at
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "enriched+monad#RelationToStrongMonads"} enriched monad – relation to Strong monads
          %p
            One possible reason why it may be hard to grasp the notion of strength is because in the case
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T: Set \to Set
            has one, in fact a canonical one! This will make more sense in a moment.
          %p
            Strengths are easier to understand by considering the case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            in itself, and one of the most important aspects of strengths is this:
          %ul
            %li
              A functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T: V \to V
              with a strength is the same thing as a
              = succeed "-enrichment" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            Here’s how that works. An enrichment on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            consists of a natural family of morphisms in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(a, b) \to hom(T(a), T(b)),
          %p
            satisfying some suitable axioms. Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom
            denotes the enrichment of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            in itself – the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            to a map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(a, b) \otimes T(a) \to T(b)
          %p
            and now the strength allows you to slide
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(a, b)
            inside
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            to the evaluation:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi eval
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ,
                          %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(hom(a, b) \otimes a) \overset{T(eval_{a, b})}{\to} T(b)
          %p
            and presto, you’re done. The strength axioms ensure that this enrichment structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            satisfies the axioms you need for a functor to be enriched.
          %p (And going back the other way, from an enrichment to a strength, is also easy – there you have to dualize. That is, instead of using the evaluation which is the counit of the hom-tensor adjunction, you use the coevaluation which is the unit. )
          %p After some work, one can convince oneself that the notions of strength and enrichment for endofunctors on closed monoidal categories really are equivalent notions.
          %p
            And now we can understand why strengths in the case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = Set
            are so “invisible” – every functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T: Set \to Set
            is
            = succeed "-enriched;" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            (Plenty of examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monads"} strong monads
            are of interest, particularly in the context of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+in+computer+science"} monads in computer science
          %p
          #ForHeytingAlgebras.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              Suppose the theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebras"} Boolean algebras
              – say we’re studying the structure of a free Boolean algebra on a set of generators,
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B[X]
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \colon B[X] \to B[X]
            %p
              in the theory, such as
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo =
                      %mi p
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T = p \wedge (-)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo =
                      %mi p
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T = p \Rightarrow (-)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+S.+Peirce"} Charles S. Peirce
              is that any definable unary operator in the theory of Boolean algebras carries a strength, or if you prefer is enriched. That may be taken to be the essence of Peirce’s iteration rule for Alpha, and it
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} categorifies
              right over to a similar statement for the theory of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} star-autonomous categories
            %p (Peirce went a little further, and incorporated notions of contravariant strength as well.)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+natural+transformation"} strong natural transformation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+functor"} cartesian closed functor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monad"} strong monad
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensorial+costrength"} tensorial costrength
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative monad
          %h2#references References
          %p The notion of tensorial strength originates in
          %ul
            %li#Kock72
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
              , below Thm. 1.3 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Strong functors and monoidal monads
              %strong 23
              (1972) 113–120 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01304852"}> doi:10.1007/BF01304852
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://home.imf.au.dk/kock/SFMM.pdf"} pdf
          %p Comprehensive review:
          %ul
            %li#Ratkovic12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kruna+S.+Ratkovic"}> Kruna S. Ratkovic
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Tensorial Strength
              %em Morita theory in enriched context
              (2012) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.2774"}> arXiv:1302.2774
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://theses.hal.science/tel-00785301/document"} hal:tel-00785301
          %p
            Review in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+in+computer+science"} monads in computer science
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarmo+Uustalu"}> Tarmo Uustalu
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Strong functors, Strong monads
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "https://www.ioc.ee/~tarmo/ssgep15/"} Containers for effects and contexts
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.ioc.ee/~tarmo/ssgep15/ssgep-1a.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Uustalu-StrongFunctors.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#McDermottUustalu22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dylan+McDermott"}> Dylan McDermott
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarmo+Uustalu"} Tarmo Uustalu
                = succeed "," do
                  %em What Makes a Strong Monad?
                %strong 360
                (2022) 113-133 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.00851"}> arXiv:2207.00851
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4204/EPTCS.360.6"} doi:10.4204/EPTCS.360.6
          %p The tentative ‘more conceptual’ definition of tensorial strength, as well as the ‘description’ above, arose in this discussion:
          %ul
            %li#Baez09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              %em The Monads Hurt My Head – But Not Anymore
              (
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/07/the_monads_hurt_my_head_but_no.html#c025476"}> blog
              )
